# answered by claude-opus-5-5, prompt /lib/phil/senses f4c9d13865f3, 2026-10-19
heat	one
pain	one
perceives	one
# pain: one could read S1's "a very great pain" as the heat merely causing pain in a perceiver, rather than being a pain that something undergoes. That reading would split "subject of pain" between being the cause of pain and suffering pain. But the text treats the heat as itself a pain, so "subject" consistently means the bearer in which it is felt.
